Rewritten: the whole Python plugin around the endpoint is Lisp here — the ticket format and
its offline validation (scheme, endpoint id, secret length, fifteen-minute expiry, one-use
nonce), the profile-scoped peer registry and nonce store with their 0600 files, the outbound
redaction of credential-shaped tokens, the JSON-RPC round trip to `hermes-iroh-sidecar serve`,
the five `iroh_peer_*` tools, the operator's panel and the manual. The ticket format and the
state layout are the original's, so an agent running either implementation can pair with and
call one running the other. Left out: the file-transfer tools (`iroh_send_file`,
`iroh_fetch_file`, `iroh_transfer_status`), which drive the separate `send_hermes` CLI and
its tracked background provider; the platform adapter that feeds inbound tasks into a Hermes
gateway's event loop (a Nodecode cell has no such adapter, and a task arrives here as a call
from a paired agent); and the Rust sidecar itself, which is kept and driven rather than
rewritten — this folder bundles no binary, it names one in the config.
